Here's a basic CartComponent that does nothing except render the number of items in the cart:

```jsx
import { useSelector, useDispatch } from'react-redux';
import { cartSlice } from './cartSlice';
import { Item } from './Item';

const CartComponent = () => {
  const count = useSelector((state) => state.cart.items.length);
  const dispatch = useDispatch();

  const handleRemove = (id) => dispatch(cartSlice.actions.removeItem(id));
  const handleAdd = (item) => dispatch(cartSlice.actions.addItem(item));

  return (
    <div className="cart">
      <h2>Cart: {count}</h2>
      <ul>
        {useSelector((state) => state.cart.items).map((item) => (
          <li key={item.id}>
            <Item item={item} onRemove={handleRemove} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
};
```

This is the minimal possible implementation. A real cart component would probably include quantity controls and a checkout button. You'd also need to make sure that the Item component correctly removes the item when the remove button is clicked, but it's assumed that the Item component pulls its 'onRemove' prop and dispatches the cart action itself.

