This code assumes you've connected your store with your CartComponent using a top-level `<Provider store={store}>` wrapping your app component. Then, your CartComponent would map over the reducer's state, dispatching actions into the store whenever it needs some piece of state changed. The most common way to wire up CartComponent with the store is using `useSelector` for making requests for state, and `useDispatch` for dispatching actions whenever it needs state updated.

Here's a basic `CartComponent` with quantity controls and a checkout button, integrated with Redux Toolkit's `useSelector` and `useDispatch` hooks.

```jsx
import React from'react';
import { useSelector, useDispatch } from'react-redux';
import { addItem, removeItem } from './cartSlice.js';

const CartComponent = () => {
  const { items } = useSelector(state => state.cart);
  const dispatch = useDispatch();

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Cart</h1>
      <ul>
        {items.map(item => (
          <li key={item.id}>
            <span>{item.name}, {item.price}</span>
            <button onClick={() => dispatch(removeItem(item.id))}>
              Remove
            </button>
            <QuantityControl quantity={item.quantity} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
};

const QuantityControl = ({ quantity }) => {
  const dispatch = useDispatch();
  return (
    <select value={quantity} onChange={e => dispatch(
      quantity === 1? addItem(item) : removeItem(item.id)
    )}>
      {[1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map(q => (
        <option key={q} value={q}>
          +{q}
        </option>
      ))}
    </select>
  );
};
```

This is pretty straightforward, but the quantity control piece — which dispatches `addItem` when the quantity is bumped up, `removeItem` when it's bumped down — ought to be handled with separate actions and selectors, not this mix of discrete actions and some-magic counting.

